        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/7DYNGM52>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/AFU4WZ6V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/ALZHNC5R>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/C5VSFIT5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/DLNK78FE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/E42L8EBK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/H8BPZXRP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/HAIV9NB4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/HPGFM6AW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/J4DUYND8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/JNF2XZEE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/K96DXSWN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/KSEWDZWR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/MJCQQAK4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/NA7KQ6ZL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/NVJXIKTT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/RZEKGEJ6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/TK7H77AI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/TP6KNIS7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/ZGXYZXND>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/TCA24-ch3-1069> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """According to this approach, transformative change occurs collaboratively through the co-creation of knowledge on pathways, strategies and visions that spur transformative action (Ansell et al., 2022; Caniglia et al., 2020; Turnhout & Lahsen, 2022). This approach seeks to embed processes of societal change in careful, collaborative and socially-engaged knowledge production (Sellberg et al., 2021) by working at the interface of views and practices as key entry points to transformative change. Theories in this approach therefore call for a transformation in how research and education are produced and governed (Horcea-Milcu et al., 2024) and are explored further in Strategies 4 and 5 in Chapter 5. Knowledge co-creation-based approaches aim to improve the science-society interface and to better integrate knowledge within decision-making (Pascual, Balvanera, Anderson et al., 2023).

Examples under this approach are transdisciplinary projects (Lang et al., 2012), participatory action research (Arnstein, 1969; Blackstock et al., 2007), ethnoecology (Caillon et al., 2017; Gavin et al., 2015; Sabinot, 2021; Toledo & Alarcón-Cháires, 2012) and sustainability science (O’Brien, 2012; Pereira et al., 2019; Tengö et al., 2017) (Annex 3.3). While participatory approaches and forms of participatory democracy have been around for a while in policy processes, typical theories within the knowledge co-creation approach envision that co-creation starts in a bottom-up manner from a science-society partnership led by academics and/or non-academics (e.g., municipality staff, civil society representatives, or community members), or even originating from grassroots movements (Ansell et al., 2022). Co-creation can emerge from hybrid alliances which bring the historically marginalized perspectives of Indigenous Peoples and other groups to the fore (Mehta et al., 2021). Co-creation may emerge from processes designed to imagine just and sustainable futures (visioning), develop alternative pathways towards these desirable futures or co-design context-specific and legitimate solution options (Chapter 2). Examples include the three-horizon technique (Sharpe et al., 2016) or arts-based methods. Examples of such experimental settings include place-based transdisciplinary fora, real-world laboratories, action-oriented projects in higher education and grassroots innovations.

Co-creation promotes the expression of a plurality of actors’ agency as a critical ingredient in transformation. The knowledge co-creation process and actions pay attention to weaving knowledge from diverse academic and non-academic sources and to cultivating adaptability and emergence to the wants and needs of involved actors (Tengö et al., 2017, Aksoy and Öz, 2020). The co-creation process seeks to build individual and collective capacities to initiate or reinforce pathways and futures collectively deemed emancipatory and desirable. The outcomes of the co-creation process include capacity development, policy uptake, institution building, management practices, networking, reframing (e.g., problems, power, agency or solutions), empowerment and social equity (Chambers et al., 2021). Moreover, co-creation approaches can set the basis for actions and interventions grounded in other approaches (e.g., empowerment, inner transformation) by contributing to levelling out power asymmetries, expanding actors’ sense of agency and inner worlds and potentially improving social inclusion.

Co-creation enables transformation by reframing conventional mainstream perspectives concerning biodiversity and valuation of ecosystem services to include, among others, marginalized and Indigenous perspectives. These include prior beliefs, assumptions and taken-for-granted perspectives on oneself, relationships with others and society. These standpoints are explored and sometimes confronted to evolve into more open, aware, collaborative and adaptable viewpoints, values and social practices. Visioning, dialogues, reflection and feedback sessions, including sharing knowledge in an accessible way, can all support this process. Various bodies of knowledge and research communities can bring their contributions to the co-creation process. For example, according to environmental history, deliberative transformative change can occur when all actors involved in an environmental conflict become aware of the role they have played in environmental degradation and this awareness is coupled with shared environmental memory transmitted through social communication and education which results in actions towards sustainable outcomes (Burke & Pomeranz, 2009; Gadgil & Guha, 1995).

Under this approach, all actors relevant to the sustainability problem at hand are called to join forces in co-creating transformations. Inclusivity and plurality of knowledge systems are highly encouraged (Fazey et al., 2018; Norström et al., 2020), as long as there is transparency at the normative level. Depending on the context and on the problem constellation (Lam, Martín-López et al., 2021), examples of relevant actors are: Indigenous Peoples and local communities, local institutional actors, researchers from academic institutions, practitioners, policymakers in international, governmental and non-governmental organizations and industry, educators and students, media, workers and trade unions, citizens, non-human communities and the private sector. The diversity of actors brings different levels of power, dependence on nature’s contributions to people, experiences and expertise. Structured ways of involving diverse actors in co-creation processes (e.g., sequence of engagement methods and tools) can greatly facilitate co-creation approaches.

In conclusion, transformative change, according to the knowledge co-creation approach, happens when various actors collaborate, when alliances among groups with different knowledge, power and access to resources are forged (e.g., local communities, community groups, scientists, youth), enabling learning that leads to the emergence of creative ways to foster transformative change and that can facilitate and enable other approaches (e.g., empowerment, inner transformation, structural). Thus, collaborative knowledge generation, grassroots visioning and other transformative science-society initiatives that couple knowledge to action are seen as key actions for navigating transformative change.

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge co-creation approaches" .

