        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/XVJ9AX8T> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The fifth insight is that a wide variety of actors, including individuals, groups and formal institutions, are able to initiate and contribute to transformative change. Different actors have different spheres of influence that interact and are dynamic (Berdegué et al., 2015). Successful transformative change is often realized by diverse actor coalitions that bring together complementary resources, capacities and approaches. The underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline (Chapter 1) and the challenges to transformative change (Chapter 4) are significant, cross-scalar and interrelated. Transformative change involves addressing these causes and challenges in an integrative manner, as emphasized especially by systems approaches (Section 3.2.1). Actor coalitions therefore play an important role in successful examples of transformative change (Enfors-Kautsky et al., 2021) (Section 3.4).

The formation of actor coalitions and networks can be facilitated by high-quality knowledge co-creation approaches (Keck & Sikkink, 1998a, 1998b; Rogowski, 1990; Sabatier, 1998) (Section 3.2.5) particularly if they pay attention to issues of power and are aligned with the principles of equity and justice, pluralism and inclusion, respectful and reciprocal human-nature relationships and adaptive learning and action. Actor coalitions are most successful when there is an alignment of values and interests (Sabatier & Weible, 2007; Sell & Prakash, 2004). Visioning processes that lead to jointly owned and agreed-upon future visions can facilitate the identification and development of shared values and interests and can be powerful in bringing together diverse groups, including those that may be in conflict (Kahane, 2004) (Chapter 2).

Research on social change highlights how the aggregation of distributed actions by individuals and institutions have contributed to transformative changes (Finnemore & Sikkink, 1998). Social movements often work for changes in structures (ways of organizing, regulating, governing) and many initiatives also focus on changing social and cultural norms (ways of doing, behaving, relating). Transformation can be fostered or nudged both through formal and informal strategies and through transformative alliances and knowledge co-creation between diverse actors that come together to co-produce transformative change and reconfigure relationships of power and knowledge (Annex 3.3).

Such collective agency is a crucial form of power for breaking down old systems and creating new systems (Charli-Joseph et al., 2018, 2023; Otto et al., 2020). Collective agency occurs when people act together, such as in social movements (Caniglia et al., 2020; Falla et al., 2024). Collective agency is situational; it is not held by groups but empowered through the relationships and resources available to actors. Enabling diverse forms of agency, especially from below, can be very powerful and build momentum for transformative change (Section 3.2.4). However, attention needs to be paid to ensuring sustainability of the change and that the change can be scaled up and out from a particular local or national context.

There can also be limits to coalitions for change; they may come together and be successful for a period of time, but in the absence of renewal, tend to be co-opted by elites (Gergan, 2020), become dormant (Durand & Sundberg, 2022), or are subsumed by other coalitions more adept at moving across scales, undermining their original goals (Lyons, 2023). Affective relations show great potential for helping create coalitions for change, at least in the short term. As Nightingale et al. (2022) show, a shared sense of grief and desires for a future for their children allowed diverse people to work together for climate change adaptation. But these efforts are always transitory. Transformation involves continual engagement, negotiation of complex relations of power and history and a radical reimagination of how inclusions and exclusions are addressed by society (Scoones et al., 2020).

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insight 5: Everyone can contribute" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/TCA24-KM12> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/TCA24-B10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/TCA24-B11>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/TCA24-B12>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/TCA24-B9> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Transformative change involves questioning the individual and collective paradigms and cultural narratives that perpetuate the underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline (Action 5.2). This can be done by recognizing and promoting world views and values that emphasize care, reciprocity and harmony with nature, including Mother Earth. These world views and values include those associated with Indigenous and local knowledge systems. For example, unquestioned habits and social norms around consumption and growth reinforce socioeconomic disparities and prevent transformative change by disrupting human-nature relationships. Social norms that define acceptable behaviours within specific contexts can rapidly shift, “denormalizing” certain practices (e.g., single-use plastics) and normalizing others (e.g., reusable containers) in the interest of transformative change. Shifts in social norms can contribute to widespread behavioural changes (Action 5.3) {B9} and can be promoted by governmental policy tools (e.g., regulations). Transformative learning can be facilitated by integrating humans’ connectedness to nature into education, health, spatial planning, communication and art, and by fostering the understanding that human well-being and quality of life are dependent on nature (Action 5.4) {B10}. For example, educational curricula, from primary to higher education, can include content on biodiversity, its loss, nature’s contributions to people, including ecosystem services, nature-based solutions and ecosystem-based approaches, and Mother Earth-centric actions to strengthen this connection. In addition, practices such as nature appreciation, systems thinking, empathy, mindfulness and transdisciplinary approaches can help to embed nature’s values into decision-making. Knowledge co-creation and recognition of multiple forms of knowledge, world views and values are crucial for developing actionable and inclusive biodiversity and sustainability strategies (Action 5.5) {B11}. Examples include the consideration of ancestral, embodied and experiential knowledge and non-human perceptions and perspectives in conservation decision-making. Context-specific, timely and dynamic communication strategies, notably through media, including social media, also play a critical role in shifting societal views and values {B12}." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shifting dominant societal views and values to recognize and prioritize human-nature interconnectedness is a powerful strategy for transformative change. These shifts can be facilitated through cultural narratives and by changing dominant social norms, facilitating transformative learning processes, co-creating new knowledge and weaving together different knowledge systems, world views and values that recognize human-nature interdependencies and ethics of care (Strategy 5, Action 5.1) {B9, B10, B11, B12}." .

