    ipbes:hasDescription """Systems approaches refer to theories that emphasize that transformative change can occur through a variety of mechanisms that influence the relationships between different parts of a system, including human-environment interactions and interconnections between different sectors. These theories may focus on specific sectors (e.g., urban, agricultural or energy systems) or on society and be applied across a range of spatial and temporal scales. Systems approaches emphasize interventions that focus on changes in structures and practices and recognize how these are related to and influenced by the overall goals of the system.

Systems approaches typically recognize that nature and human societies are deeply interconnected and that systemic dynamics can give rise to unexpected, non-linear emergent effects (Gunderson & Holling, 2003). These effects are difficult to predict and can create substantial obstacles to transformative change (Chapter 4) but can also be harnessed to accelerate transformative change (Westley et al., 2011). For example, the Fridays for Future movement started as a localized initiative but has spread across the world, raising awareness and placing pressure on governments and businesses in many countries (Wallis & Loy, 2021). Importantly, this means that while transformative change can be planned and guided, it cannot be executed according to a blueprint. Instead, it requires continuous and iterative monitoring, evaluation, learning and adaptation to sense and adjust to emerging pathways of change. During any transformative change process unanticipated outcomes and effects are almost certain to arise, potentially requiring adaptation of the approaches being implemented or introduction of new ones (Rijswijk et al., 2015; Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021). The systems approach, with its attention to relationships between people and nature as system components, aligns with Indigenous and local knowledges, which are historically-rooted and tend to recognize people-nature interconnectedness (Box 3.1).

The root causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline, according to this approach, are related to particular dynamics and relationships that reinforce patterns of behaviour, actions and structures that underlie global environmental challenges and crises, including climate change, biodiversity loss and pollution (Annex 3.3). These approaches also emphasize that these dynamics and relationships are powerfully influenced by and also shape the overall goals and paradigms of a system (Abson et al., 2017), including individual practices and beliefs. Shifting the overall goal of a system for instance, by moving away from maximizing profit or production to ensuring sustainable and just outcomes, can over time reshape the structures, dynamics and practices that lead to particular systemic outcomes) (Annex 3.3).

Systems approaches therefore point to multiple types of interventions that can help bring about transformative change. This includes through interventions affecting: i) the overall intent or goals of a system, such as maximizing production or profits; ii) the structure, rules and networks of a system that shape interactions among different actors as well as with other components of the system, including nature; and iii) feedbacks between components of a system that can dampen or reinforce change (Abson et al., 2017; Ostrom, 2009). All of these aspects influence the barriers and enabling conditions that affect actors’ agency and the feasibility of different interventions for transformation (Chapters 4 and 5).

These theories emphasize that a diversity of actors can be involved in systemic processes of transformative change by changing system structure, feedbacks and goals. Actors can come from different sectors (productive, academic, political and public), operate at different levels (local users, municipalities, regional, national or supranational) and act in bottom-up or top-down ways. Actors can include social entrepreneurs, grassroots movements, communities, youth, Indigenous Peoples, civil society organizations, non-governmental organizations, scientists, the private sector, States, governments and policymakers. More importantly, systems approaches highlight the importance of how actors are organized and the relationships among them, for example in networks and social movements as well as the role of leadership for guiding system change (Bodin, 2017; Holahan & Lubell, 2016; Villamayor-Tomas et al., 2020). In particular, to support transformative change, the efforts of different actors need to be at least partly aligned and mutually supportive and not undermine or counteract one another.

Systems approaches emphasize that there are many entry points to transforming a system, but some are likely to be more transformative than others, depending on the extent to which they affect core system dynamics (Abson et al., 2017; Leventon et al., 2022; Meadows, 1999). Systems approaches focus on the type of intervention and the part of the system that aims to be changed, suggesting that goal-setting and paradigm shifts can trigger more pronounced shifts in systems, driving systems to states more inclusive of ecological considerations than those achieved by other types of change, such as modifications in system parameters like taxes and subsidies (Meadows, 1999). This attention to the most powerful interventions in a system lends itself to the metaphor of leverage points, i.e., places or properties of systems where interventions may trigger changes in the whole system (Abson et al., 2017; Leventon et al., 2022).

Systems approaches also point to the need to navigate the complexity of different efforts to promote transformative change. In systems approaches, human agency and intentional changes operate in a mix with unintentional changes, diverse contexts, path dependencies, systemic feedbacks, emergent effects and nonlinearities, emphasizing the difficulty of fully controlling transformation in complex social-ecological systems (Charli-Joseph et al., 2018; Manuel-Navarrete & Pelling, 2015; Rip, 1995). Systems approaches also highlight that there are different phases to a transformation process and that different types of actions, capacities and leadership styles are needed during different phases of a transformative process, including work that prepares a system for transformation, helps navigate the turbulence and tensions during the key phases of change and helps consolidate and stabilize a new pathway of change (Moore et al., 2014; Olsson et al., 2004).

Systems approaches furthermore highlight the importance of cross-scale interactions in supporting transformative change. Specifically, they highlight the role of larger scale structures (e.g., rules, governance arrangements, norms, meta-narratives) in enabling or constraining the emergence and scaling of innovations at local and regional scales (Gunderson & Holling, 2003; Hebinck et al., 2022; Loorbach et al., 2017). Similarly, they highlight the potential local or small-scale innovations to disrupt prevailing systems and forge new pathways, given suitable opportunity contexts. These “windows of opportunity” often arise from crises that open up spaces for institutionalizing new ways of doing through new rules and practices (Gunderson & Holling, 2003; Hebinck et al., 2022; Loorbach et al., 2017; Moore et al., 2014; Olsson et al., 2004).

Specific examples of intentional change in systems approaches include visioning and connecting agents of change planning to drive action (Moore et al., 2014; Olsson et al., 2004; Sharpe et al., 2016), experimentation with alternatives to current societal regimes by empowering local initiatives (Pereira, Karpouzoglou et al., 2018) and social innovations that can reduce power of the current unsustainable structures and counteract lock-in (E. Bennett et al., 2016; Leventon et al., 2021).

The implications of a system perspective range from emphasizing the inherent and dynamic complexity of interconnected human and natural systems to seeing human agency as central in responding to environmental change and environmental crises (Brown & Westaway, 2011). Theories that emphasize dynamic complexity highlight the unpredictability of systems, the non-linearity of their dynamics and the importance of cross-scale interactions. All of these have consequences which may result in intended and unintended outcomes and limitations for governing transformative change (Flood, 2002; Miller & Page, 2008; Rotmans et al., 2001).

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Systems approaches" .

