        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/JBMPB9WC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/JQZXVCN3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/NX5BDJ9Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/PCBTEIWY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/Q7Y7IVF4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/RBFZBJDJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/SF6KT4GQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/TLVI3QA7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/U7DCFXEI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/ULZ32S9L>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/UPJMDFQR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WEXXEGQ5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/X53NI6HA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/X82922D7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/Y9L9G33W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/ZFAARQSJ> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The main assumption in this approach is that the starting point for transformative change lies in the inner worlds of individuals or collectives (i.e., views). Transformative change occurs when the inner dimensions of sustainability (such as beliefs and values) are linked to outer dimensions (such as actions) towards sustainability-oriented views, structures and practices. Transformative change is therefore mobilized through integrative measures that link individual and collective inner worlds. This may entail an awareness of one’s own agency (O’Brien et al., 2023) and unleashing people’s potential and capacity to care, commit to and effect change for a more sustainable life (Wamsler & Osberg, 2022). The three spheres of transformation (personal, political and practical) (Annex 3.3) provide a framework for understanding how the personal sphere shapes the way in which individuals interact with structures, power dynamics and collaborations in the political sphere (O’Brien & Sygna, 2013).

The root causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline most targeted by the inner transformation approach are disconnection from and domination over nature and people. Alienation from self, others and nature is considered to underpin contemporary social-ecological polycrisis while playing a central part on other social-ecological challenges such as colonization of nature and people, power imbalances, a sense of hopelessness, unsustainable narratives and paradigms of separation, unsustainable individual behaviours, as well as hegemonic narratives that have desecrated nature, turning it into a commodity. These hegemonic narratives lead to internalized messages of separation, superiority and instrumentalization in relation to nature. To uproot them, one first step is for individuals to become aware of their existence and how they are perpetuated. Transformative efforts thus focus on transformations of particular mindsets, beliefs, values, worldviews, paradigms, cultures and associated inner cognitive, emotional and relational capacities that translate into transformations in exterior worlds, such as habits, practices, governance or welfare policies. (Shrivastava et al., 2020; Wamsler, 2020) (Annex 3.3).

Actions associated with this approach include investing in relationships and working on the relationship with oneself. Relational activities that nurture human and other-than-human relationships, intra- and inter-generational relationships, self-other relationships and relationships with oneself can come from numerous domains such as environmental psychology, moral philosophy or from practices such as meaningful, sensory and emotional engagement with nature (Sheffield et al., 2022). Mindfulness, compassion training, self-awareness and inner reflexivity exercises, personal development and deep practices have the potential to mobilize, activate or shift individual consciousness, mindsets and values and ultimately people’s decision-making process to orientate them towards just and empathetic relationships and futures. Other mechanisms for addressing inner worlds may include developing practices of introspection in higher education and learning (Fazey et al., 2021; Woiwode, 2020) and the integration of transformative qualities/capacities and universal values (such as compassion) as explicit aims/criteria in regional, national or local performance frameworks, rather than economic growth (European Environment Agency, 2023; Wamsler & Bristow, 2022).

Examples from the literature focus on repairing and regenerating human-nature relationships, nature connectedness, humans’ affective relationship with nature and the extent to which humans see themselves as part of nature (Lumber et al., 2017; Mayer & Frantz, 2004; Richardson, Dobson, et al., 2020; Richardson, Passmore, et al., 2020). This literature emphasizes moving away from human-nature relationships characterized by utility, control and duality to mutually constitutive human- and more-than-human relationships (Scarano, 2024). Indigenous and local knowledge can play an important role in these theories, as these often express worldviews more representative of interconnection, balance and harmony (IPBES, 2022; Wamsler & Bristow, 2022). Bodies of knowledge on inner relationships and perspectives on oneself (e.g., positive psychology, Raymond & Raymond, 2019) and on spirituality (Ives & Kidwell, 2019) also contribute to this approach with activities that help people tap into their inner potential. This includes practices such as contemplative techniques, psychological and cognitive-behavioural based techniques, transformative facilitation, communication and coaching techniques (Wamsler, Hertog, et al., 2021; Wamsler, Osberg, et al., 2021).

However, the impact of these techniques is limited at the level of individual’s views, without corresponding changes in practices and structures (Purser, 2021; Watson et al., 2023; Woroniecki et al., 2024). Theories in this approach counter this limitation by signalling that agency is better represented as distributed across individual and collective actors and actions. Inner transformation is not about fixing people’s views (Ives et al., 2023; Wamsler, Osberg, et al., 2021) but instead creating integrative measures, spaces and conditions that nurture a culture of inner development, mutual support and engagement. Education is an important example in this approach, that provides knowledge, spaces for deliberation and opportunities for practising civic engagement in environmental issues (Hayward, 2020). Such integration can be supported through working environments, communities of practice, social movements, aesthetics and education. The latter involves holistic, contemplative, psychological, cognitive-behavioural and ethical learning approaches, arts- and nature-based methods that nurture self-reflection, intrinsic value orientation, compassion, and individual and collective agency (Hedlund-de Witt et al., 2014; Wamsler, 2018).

By cultivating regenerative dialogues between different worldviews and different forms of interpretation of reality, a permanently evolving understanding of both inner and outer worlds can be weaved (Scarano, 2024; Woiwode et al., 2021). Seeking out alternatives, often non-western relational ontologies, helps overcome longstanding rigid dichotomies between nature and culture, mind and matter, subject and object, which have long shaped modern worldviews. Diverse, often Indigenous knowledge systems are not premised on such divides (Walsh et al., 2021), emphasizing the process nature of life, i.e., that the ‘world exists in a perpetual state of ‘becoming’, comprising continually unfolding relations (S. West et al., 2020).

Individuals and their relationships with one another and non-human beings are the main actors of inner transformations. Transitional and broader social movements involving marginalized groups such as Indigenous Peoples and local communities, women and students are also considered elements of change. Organizations that shape mindsets are also important, including the United Nations, private, non-profit and faith-based organizations, educational institutions, citizen groups, Indigenous Peoples and local communities and people engaged in collective, embodied and reciprocal practices across the public realm.

In conclusion, inner transformation for sustainability is a field of research, education and practice which recognizes the interdependence of inner and outer phenomena across views, structures and practices; the importance of addressing inner dimensions across individuals and collectives; and the potential that is latent within every human to enable transformative change.

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inner transformation approaches" .

