        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WU8EJH3R> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The third insight is that visions play a powerful role in transformative change by giving space for imagination, providing inspiration, motivating collective action and reshaping behaviour at both individual and societal scales (Chapter 2). They can be used to disrupt the views, structures and practices that have led to the current crisis and to expand and embed equitable and sustainable alternatives. Transformative change implies a willingness to experiment and do things differently (Pereira, Hichert et al., 2018). Approaches that reinforce or conform to existing challenges and their manifestations constitute complexes of interrelated values, ontologies, behaviour, knowledges, extraction and production practices, government institutions, regulations and actor networks (Amel et al., 2017; Boström et al., 2018; Heyen & Wolff, 2019; Sewell, 2005). These current conditions are entrenched and hard to change for a number of reasons, including their complex interdependencies; powerful actors and institutions that profit from the status quo and resist change (Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021); and partly because of the lack of clear visions and imaginations of viable alternative ways of living (E. Bennett et al., 2016; Martin et al., 2022). These relationships can be questioned and new approaches can be nurtured.

A number of theories highlight the critical role of visions and imagination in deliberately engaging in processes of transformative change (Chapter 2). Imagining alternative possible futures can inform policy, mobilize resources, inspire individual agency and collective action and reshape behaviour at both individual and societal scales (Mansur et al., 2022; Moore & Milkoreit, 2020; Riedy & Waddock, 2022; Stoetzler & Yuval-Davis, 2002) (Section 3.2). Feminist political ecologists have argued for the importance of imagination to enact desired relations, exchanges and values in the present, recognizing the histories of injustice and environmental harm that shape the future and kinship with all of life (Gergan, 2020; Lyons, 2023). However, many current policy targets reveal an imagination gap (Chapter 2), as they are based on implicit futures that largely assume a continuation of the status quo, with solutions to current sustainability crises revolving around narrow scientific and economic directions (McPhearson et al., 2021; Swilling, 2013) (Chapter 1). Carefully designed participatory imagination processes can build alternative, more diverse, collectively-owned visions (Box 3.2, Annex 3.1; Chapter 2), drawing on and integrating knowledge co-creation, empowerment and systems approaches to transformative change. Such alternative visions are increasingly seen as critical to developing shared future goals and understanding what needs to change, as well as to mobilizing the collective action and resources needed to achieve transformative change (E. Bennett et al., 2016; Lembi et al., 2024; Martin et al., 2022).

Disruption of unsustainable views, structures and practices can be achieved in different ways, but often entails targeting systems of power relationships (Linnér & Wibeck, 2019b; Luederitz et al., 2017; Scoones et al., 2020; Sewell, 2005; Temper, Walter et al., 2018). Transformation is not an end state, rather it is a radical reshaping of current social, political, economic-environmental relations through which change occurs (Di Chiro, 2021; Lyons, 2023). Crises such as pandemics that disrupt and challenge everyday practices can also create windows of opportunity for transformative change (Herrfahrdt-Pähle et al., 2020; Pahl-Wostl et al., 2023). Potentially transformative actions can involve mobilizing and redistributing resources, capacities and knowledge to create relationships able to disrupt unsustainable extraction, exploitation and pollution and promote restorative and regenerative actions (Chapter 5). Such actions draw, for example, on empowerment (Section 3.2.4), knowledge co-creation (Section 3.2.5) and science and technology approaches (Section 3.2.6). Transformative disruptions at the community-level, spatially located, over time, can spread out promoting pockets of change all over a country, a continent, or even globally (Section 3.5.6). Examples include the ecovillage, plant-based diets, Fridays for Future and flying less movements (Wallis & Loy, 2021). Often, these movements are coupled with inner transformation at a personal level that reverberate at the collective level (Section 3.2.3). Transformative disruptions may also start at a global or national level promoted by structural change (Section 3.2.3), for example by global or national law and cascade down to lower levels as in the case of the Montreal Protocol on Substances that Deplete the Ozone Layer (Annex 3.1). The time scale needed for transformative processes may vary from a few years to centuries (Section 3.4, Box 3.1 and 3.3, Annex 3.1).

The three horizons model (Sharpe et al., 2016) (Annex 3.3) and related X-curve (Hebinck et al., 2022) and the Seeds of Good Anthropocenes Theory of Change (Pereira, Bennett, et al., 2018)) (Annex 3.3), provide useful frameworks for understanding the role of visions and the processes of disruption, innovation and systemic change associated with transformations (Figure 3.9). These frameworks highlight the crucial role that compelling future visions that articulate alternative values, assumptions, governance arrangements and outcomes can offer in providing a sense of direction and purpose to inspire and guide transformative change (Sharpe et al., 2016; Wiek & Iwaniec, 2014). They also recognize the gap between the present world in crisis and the aspirational future, highlighting the transitional but often turbulent space for experimentation and learning needed to reach the envisioned future. This understanding of the disruptive dynamics associated with transformative change can help inform strategic actions needed to navigate uncertainty, overcome barriers and scale innovations (Sharpe et al., 2016). The three horizons and associated models provide an intuitive framework for understanding and navigating transformative change across different scales. They can be powerfully used in participatory processes to build shared visions, understanding of a system and the critical areas in need of change by providing symbolic stories that support agency and actions towards making imagined futures real (Pereira, Hichert, et al., 2018; Sharpe et al., 2016).

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insight 3: Envisioning and growing sustainable futures" .

