        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/M5NCVVRZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/NR79VV84>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/PPXTJD9F>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/RFK9ZQBE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/TGF9B8GN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/VU7HC3PD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/W6Q99TIY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WP9CDG4A>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/X2BASN4Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/XGKVLMZA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/XYAZNKRZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/TCA24-ch3-752> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Under this approach, science and technology are seen as the primary drivers of transformative change. Theoretical frameworks in the science and technology approach suggest that production of scientific and technical knowledge on biodiversity, clean production technologies and the development of socio-technical infrastructures to address biodiversity loss and nature’s decline, are essential to bring about transformative change for a just and sustainable world. While frameworks within this approach typically focus on traditional technological advances, some include the scientific development of inclusive, deliberative and democratic innovation and knowledge processes (Annex 3.3). The technological development and innovation that features in these theories often draws on traditional ecological knowledge, associated practices and biological resources that have been preserved and maintained through Indigenous and local knowledge.

Science and technology approaches recognize that there is a close, two-way interaction between policy and innovation. Policy change can trigger innovation (e.g., when technology to advance solar panel development is supported by policies to promote renewable energy), while innovation itself can trigger policy change (e.g., the Montreal Protocol on Ozone Depletion was made possible by innovations allowing the exchange of chlorofluorocarbons for other technologies) (see Case 7 outlined in Annex 3.1). Promoting transformative change through science and technology entails interactions with a diversity of actors who can take up innovations or novel technologies and spread them enough to be effective at generating transformative change (Heeks et al., 2013; Radjou et al., 2015).

Innovation, according to the science and technology approach, can take several forms. It can occur through conventional research, development, demonstration and diffusion policies. It can also be geared towards economic growth (Schumpeter, 1967), or emerge to respond to and address problems emanating from crisis, such as pandemics, socioeconomic inequalities and environmental problems. For example, agroecological transformations create integrated land-use systems that improve ecological processes and strengthen local communities, addressing problems emanating from conventional agricultural practices that rely on synthetic inputs and short-term productivity (Andrieu & Kebede, 2020). Innovation may also focus on policies or the creation of spaces for bottom-up innovation directed towards sustainability (Schot & Steinmueller, 2018). These innovations in socio-technical infrastructures and practices can provide opportunities for reshaping views and structures, such as ways of knowing and governance arrangements (Beaulieu, 2022) and thus facilitate other approaches such as structural or inner transformation approaches.

Many theoretical frameworks in the science and technology approach focus on triggering transformative change via targeted funding for research to address environmental issues, support for environmentally driven start-ups and close collaboration between academia, government and the business sector to identify areas where science and technology can address sustainability problems. However, the theoretical frameworks within this approach span those that view science and technology as universalist and neutral, following a linear process from discovery to societal impact (Asafu-Adjaye et al., 2015), to approaches that emphasize the social and cultural embeddedness and political contingencies of science and technology (Bijker, 2017; Stokland et al., 2023; Turnhout, 2018), including the importance of institutional structures and power in shaping the distribution of benefits of technological advances (Johnson & Acemoglu, 2023). These different underlying assumptions about the role of science in transformation lead to a variety of recommended actions spanning from increased funding and political uptake and support for science and technology to more inclusive, just and deliberative processes for producing and employing it (Hausknost & Haas, 2019; Levidow & Papaioannou, 2018; Mazzucato, 2021).

Key actors under this approach include scientists, universities, research institutes, science academies, epistemic communities, government agencies, the research and development branches of industry and funders (including national and transnational funding agencies, as well as non-governmental organizations, foundations and private funders of science that support innovation). Often ignored, but critically important, are Indigenous and local knowledge holders as a source of knowledge for science, technology and innovation regarding traditional knowledge, associated practices and biological resources that have been preserved and maintained through generations (Berkes, 2009, 2017; Kamau & Winter, 2013; Thrupp, 1989). Also critical are individuals who take up and use new technologies and innovations at scales sufficient to drive transformative change.

Actions to promote transformative change, according to this approach, include increased public funding and provision of incentives for research, development (Mazzucato, 2013; Mazzucato & Semieniuk, 2017; Rodrik, 2015), demonstration and diffusion of technologies directed towards reducing biodiversity loss and nature’s decline; public funding for education in science and technology; the establishment of institutionalized channels for integrating scientific knowledge on biodiversity into policymaking at multiple scales (Haas, 1992, 2015), i.e., global or national; coordination of activities between different actors (the State, universities, business and others) (Schot & Steinmueller 2018)); provision of collective and inclusive spaces for learning and societal experimentation that allows for collaboration in setting the priorities for technology and innovation by various actors (Aksoy & Öz, 2020; Schot, 2002).

The underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline are not directly targeted in this approach. Rather, it seeks to directly address policies and practices that lead to biodiversity loss and nature’s decline; social and economic inequalities caused by disruptive production processes (Pansera & Owen, 2018); exclusionary innovations (Kaplinsky et al., 2009) and exploitative agriculture practices that reduce the natural capital of agroecosystems. At times, science and technology provide insights into the nature of the problem (for example whether chlorofluorocarbons affect the ozone layer or whether carbon emissions cause climate change) and then contribute to solutions through the development of new technologies and social innovations for consumption and improved monitoring capacities (e.g., machine learning processed satellite data). However, technological innovations can also lead to unforeseen environmental, health or social impacts that lead to further innovations and interventions (Clapp & Ruder, 2020; Luque-Ayala et al., 2024). Promotion of science and technology approaches sometimes overshadows the role of Indigenous and local knowledge and other contextually-relevant solutions to environmental problems.

In conclusion, theories that fall within this approach focus on how transformative change can be navigated through the design or organization of socio-technical infrastructures to drive actions and practices that transform the way people engage with the causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline. These changes can have knock-on effects that create opportunities for other approaches. For example, the development of social technologies with the participation of people to solve their problems (Chataway et al., 2010), can strengthen their capabilities (Heeks et al., 2013; Levidow & Papaioannou, 2018; Papaioannou, 2018) and support empowerment as well as knowledge co-creation approaches. Recent potentially transformative scientific and technological developments, such as artificial intelligence, digital innovations, synthetic biology and gene-editing technologies, provide crucial points of discussion on the role and impact of these emerging technologies on economic production, human well-being and sustainability (Clapp & Ruder, 2020; Erdmann et al., 2023; German Environment Agency & Federal Agency for Nature Conservation, 2024; Ingram & Maye, 2020; Johnson & Acemoglu, 2023; Luque-Ayala et al., 2024; Nicholson & Reynolds, 2020; Rabitz et al., 2022).

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Science and technology approaches" .

