    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The sixth insight is that diffuse local sustainability initiatives can contribute to global sustainability through various scaling processes, including scaling out (e.g., replication of innovations to other geographical contexts), scaling up (e.g., institutionalizing innovations in policy, law or rules), scaling deep (e.g., shifting mindsets, paradigms or values) and forming new constellations of initiatives. Many current sustainability initiatives constitute experiments or innovations in local transformations and function as real-world laboratories or transformative spaces to develop and test context-specific solutions (Bulkeley et al., 2016; Pereira, Bennett et al., 2018; Schäpke et al., 2018; Wolfram et al., 2016), (Section 3.4). Such initiatives are often sector-specific (e.g., focused on food, energy or mobility) or limited in geographic scope (Brondízio, Andersson et al., 2021; Leventon et al., 2022; Londres et al., 2023) (Section 3.4). For transformative change to occur at the scales needed to address biodiversity loss and nature’s decline, such initiatives, experiments or innovations need to grow and connect with other innovations to have wider scale impacts that address the underlying geopolitical, macroeconomic and social-cultural causes of these challenges (Brondízio et al., 2023; Cash et al., 2006; Hermans et al., 2016; Lam, Martín-López, et al., 2020; Loorbach et al., 2020; Seyfang & Smith, 2007).

Different approaches and theories highlight different ways in which transformative change can diffuse or be scaled, e.g., from local to global, internal personal to external societal (Section 3.2). One commonly used set of scaling concepts in transformative change distinguishes between scaling out, up and deep (Lam, Martín-López, et al., 2020; Moore et al., 2015). Scaling out focuses on the replication of sustainability initiatives, experiments or innovations to occur over a wider geographical extent to affect more people (Lam, Martín-López, et al., 2020; Loorbach et al., 2020; Westley et al., 2014). For example, new sustainable technologies need to be scaled out to reach more people and replace unsustainable incumbent technologies (Schot & Geels, 2008). Scaling up refers to processes that alter higher institutional levels, by integrating new sustainable practices into laws, rules or policy (Gorissen et al., 2018; Loorbach et al., 2020; Naber et al., 2017). Scaling deep refers to processes affecting the inner worlds of people by shifting mindsets, paradigms and values (E. Bennett et al., 2016; Horcea-Milcu et al., 2019; Moore et al., 2015). Different local-scale initiatives can also combine with one another to form alternative systems or ways of doing, e.g., when community currencies combine with community-supported agriculture and smart local energy systems to produce an alternative local economy (Geels, 2002; Loorbach et al., 2020; Pereira, Bennett, et al., 2018).

During the process of scaling, different contexts may involve adaptations of technologies or implementation processes (Olsson et al., 2017; Rotmans & Loorbach, 2008). This can be facilitated by transdisciplinary approaches where local academic and non-academic actors co-create context-specific sustainability knowledge and solutions, drawing on examples from other places (Lam, Freund et al., 2021; Lang et al., 2012). Scaling processes also need to be cognisant of the complexity of social-ecological change as it is neither predictable nor controllable due to diverse contexts (Section 3.5.7). Finally, it is important to recognize and respect that some initiatives do not want, can, or need to scale as they may be highly context-specific and want to remain local or avoid being coopted by more powerful actors (Augenstein et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insight 6: Scaling transformative change" .

