        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/TCA24-ch3-912> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """There is a large and diverse set of literature grappling with how deliberate transformative change can occur. From this varied literature, 62 theories, frameworks, methods and study approaches (hereafter referred to generically as “theories”) were assessed (Sovacool & Hess, 2017), covering a wide variety of disciplines and fields of research and practice (Annex 3.3). These theories were identified in three steps: 1) experts from the chapter identified relevant theoretical frameworks that provide insights into how transformative change occurs; 2) two rounds of literature identification were conducted via an online survey with Transformative Change Assessment experts to identify additional relevant theoretical frameworks; and 3) contributing authors with specific expertise were contacted to provide detailed descriptions, summaries and references for each framework. This process resulted in 62 theories showcasing diverse perspectives on transformative change (Annex 3.3).

Based on commonalities in their underlying assumptions and understanding of how transformative change occurs and their implications for policy and practice, theories were grouped into six broad approaches to transformative change: 1) systems; 2) structural; 3) inner transformation; 4) empowerment; 5) knowledge co-creation; and 6) science and technology (Figure 3.1). Each of the six broad approaches provides unique insights to understand, describe, analyse, trigger and navigate how transformative change occurs.

The six broad approaches were inductively determined during the first step of the process while also considering existing typologies of transformation approaches. Theories and frameworks were clustered together based on commonalities in their underlying assumptions and rationales on how transformative change occurs, with special attention to how the approach understands transformative change. In this way, six approaches clustered around the emerging themes of technology, knowledge co-creation, systems, inner worlds, power and structures were derived. The subsequent adjustment, consolidation and validation of the six approaches was an iterative and reflexive process, where the delineation of the six approaches was repeatedly verified against existing and newly identified theories in the second and third steps above (Annex 3.2). Ultimately, each of the 62 identified theories was coded according to how it understands transformative change to occur, as well as the main actions, actors and primary root causes of biodiversity decline according to its particular theoretical framework. Based on this coding, each theory was assigned to one or more of the six approaches to transformative change (Figure 3.2) and each of the six approaches were mapped onto the main entry points on which they typically focus to promote processes of transformative change (Figure 3.1).

To support this assessment, an additional comprehensive analysis was conducted using key review papers and the main literature identified in step three for each approach. This strengthened the classification of the theories into the six broad approaches and highlighted potential knowledge gaps in the review (Section 3.7). As the literature on transformative change is very recent, a wide range of disciplinary fields are helpful to understand what nurtures and drives change. This chapter takes the 62 theories that emerged during the process described above as the basis for the assessment. However, there may be other theories of development, social change and organizational transformations that could also be considered as relevant to understanding transformative change.

Each of the approaches primarily emphasizes one or two of the three dimensions or entry points to transformative change – views, structures and practices – introduced in Chapter 1 (Figure 3.1). For example, the science and technology approach focuses primarily on practices, while the knowledge co-creation approach operates predominantly at the interface between practices and views.

The inner transformation approach focuses primarily on views, while the empowerment approach focuses on the interface between views and structures. Meanwhile the structural approach focuses on changes in structures as the main entry point for change and the systems approach works more directly at the interface of structures and practices. However, all approaches and entry points can trigger larger cascading effects onto other dimensions and the boundaries of influence are therefore not clear-cut. Taken together, they provide a more comprehensive understanding of the diversity of interacting ways in which transformation across the three dimensions of change can occur. Thus, transformative change entailing changes across views, structures and practices is most likely to occur through a combination of multiple, complementary approaches. Which approaches and entry points are most effective and appropriate will depend on the particular context.

The six approaches should not be understood to be independent from, nor opposing, each other; rather they represent idealized archetypes with distinctive features that provide alternative and potentially complementary insights and approaches on how to promote, accelerate and scale transformative change. The following subsections describe each of the broad approaches in more detail, highlighting the way in which they understand transformative change to occur, the key actions and interventions associated with the approach, as well as the key actors involved and the contribution of or interaction with Indigenous and local knowledge. A summary of the key features of each of the six approaches is provided in Table 3.1 and a synthesis across the approaches is provided in Section 3.3.

Notes
3. Review of knowledge on theories and frameworks of transformative change (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10250533).
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).
4. Systematic review of literature on transformative change for sustainability (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10218646).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Approaches to transformative change" .

