        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/TCA24-ch3-1075> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/TCA24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This approach holds that transformative change occurs when agency and power are asserted from currently marginalized groups in ways that transform power relations for the benefit of sustainability and equity. These approaches emphasize the interplay between societal structures and views in bringing about transformative change, radically altering dominant social and human-nature relationships (Blaikie & Brookfield, 2015; Martinez-Alier, 2003; Woroniecki et al., 2019). Change arises through intentional actions and shifts in individual and collective agency (Gaventa, 2006; VeneKlasen, 2006). Through critical self-awareness, engagement, resistance and protest, historically marginalized groups mobilize against underlying causes of biodiversity loss rooted in environmental injustices and entrenched powers to foster equitable and sustainable futures (Falla et al., 2024; Nightingale, 2017; Woroniecki, 2020). These actions address underlying social injustices and power dynamics, leveraging diverse and marginalized knowledges, perspectives, values and experiences to catalyze change (Pascual, Balvanera, & Christie, 2023). Transformative change manifests through new social arrangements, contested political spaces and empowered grassroots innovations that reconfigure dominant development trajectories and material flows (Drydyk, 2008; Eyben et al., 2006; Harcourt & Nelson, 2015) (Annex 3.3).

Theories within this approach include an emancipatory understanding of transformation linked to empowerment drawing on Paulo Freire’s (2000) notion of praxis, which is both a critique of existing social arrangements and a search for alternatives (Kabeer, 1999; Mehta et al., 2021). Theories in this approach emphasize values such as justice, equity and sustainability and focus on plurality and multiple dimensions of environmental justice (e.g., distributive, recognition and procedural) (Schlosberg, 2007, 2013; Temper, Demaria et al., 2018) as well as on the notion of capabilities (Nussbaum, 2002, 2011; Polishchuk & Rauschmayer, 2012; Sen, 2001, 2008). This approach envisages multiple means and actions for empowerment of actors (Stirling, 2015; Temper, Walter, et al., 2018). Mobilization and organization by Indigenous Peoples, local communities, minorities and other marginalized peoples can be a key feature of transformative change, through strategies such as lobbying, direct action, protest, strategic alliances with multiple actors and capacity-building (Ahmed, 2001; IPBES, 2023; Woroniecki et al., 2019). Other actions include fostering social movements and building grassroots networks, envisioning alternative pathways through participatory processes rooted in community ideas and values and work to gain recognition and representation in key arenas of power.

During processes of transformative change, both individuals and diverse social groups acquire agency through collective action (McLaughlin & Dietz, 2008) in complex decision-making, bargaining and participatory processes. Bargaining, negotiation, non-action, resistance and reflection are important expressions of agency, in addition to decision-making (Kabeer, 1999; Manlosa, 2022). Feminist scholars have played a key role in theorising about agency, empowerment and transformative change for sustainability in ways that promote gender and social justice. Some decolonial and feminist approaches focus on environmental justice, gender equality and decolonization simultaneously and call for dismantling the naturalized patterns of power and domination that exploit nature and marginalized communities (Burgart Goutal, 2020; Cumes, 2009; Curiel Pichardo, 2007) and focus on care as a set of ethical practices that allow human and environmental well-being to flourish (Mehta & Harcourt, 2021). Social and environmental justice movements rooted in these traditions thus advocate for just transformations that challenge dominant systems of power and exploitation by recognizing the interconnectedness of social, environmental and political issues (Mies & Shiva, 2004) (Figure 3.5; Annex 3.3). The Environmental Justice Atlas (Global Atlas of Environmental Justice, n.d.) documents stories and movements of Indigenous Peoples and local communities from around the world mobilizing and struggling against biodiversity loss and environmental injustices arising through extractive activities and infrastructure projects.

By centring the experiences, struggles and knowledge of Indigenous Peoples, women and other marginalized groups, empowerment approaches attempt to support diverse communities and peoples in their efforts to achieve meaningful participation in decision-making processes related to defining and implementing processes of transformative change (López-Serrano, 2023). These actors, often silenced or oppressed, emerge as key agents through participatory processes, grassroots innovations, collective actions, social movements (Mouffe, 2022) and ‘environmentalism of the poor’ (Martinez-Alier, 2003). By fostering critical thinking, dialogue and engagement with real-world problems, education based on deliberation and active participation can be a powerful tool for empowering marginalized groups (Freire & Macedo, 2005). The interplay of actors with institutions, policymakers and scientific communities through participatory, deliberative processes creates a dynamic force challenging existing power structures and enabling transformation. Through alternative practices, social innovations and visions in the making, these actors can address direct (e.g., land-use changes, climate change, pollution) and indirect (e.g., economy, values) drivers of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline (Avelino, 2021; Avelino et al., 2023). Political ecology conceptualizes these activities as a driver of transformative change because it brings to light, politicizes and eventually overcomes prevailing social-ecological relationships (Blaikie & Brookfield, 2015; Peet & Watts, 2004).

Actors in this approach include individuals and environmental justice, feminist, Indigenous and degrowth social movements against extractive unsustainable and capitalist-driven environment-development trajectories. Specific actions include those led to assert agency and address social, gender and environmental injustices, regain access to and control over the commons and natural resources which are key to sustaining their livelihoods, assert alternative visions to nature/human/more-than-human relations in decision-making spaces, and to reverse dominant systems of exploitation of nature and humans (Harcourt & Nelson, 2015; Temper, Walter, et al., 2018).

In conclusion, transformative change, according to this approach, happens when marginalized people regain self-determination vis a vis dominant and exploitation power structures. Power is not simply power ‘over’ but also ‘power to’ and power ‘with’, power ‘within’ and power ‘for’ (Lécuyer et al., 2024; VeneKlasen & Miller, 2002). Through these reconfigurations of power, hitherto disempowered and marginalized groups use critical praxis, self-reflection, collective action and agency to gain concrete achievements through changing structures, asserting identities, gaining representation, recognition and rights in legal and other formal structures, often leading to better biodiversity outcomes (Annex 3.3).

Notes
2. See glossary (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11382223).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Empowerment approaches" .

