        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/Q4LDTBU3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/Q9QNQ7FV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/QC2NHHXR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/QF5V85X6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/R368UVY4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/R46V5232>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/UNPCJQBU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/URKNDAWW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/VA3Z74S2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WACSCNAS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WBLFB8BJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/WCNU7ZBV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/XWNI8NAE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/XZSUGRTM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4589462/items/Z94DB3UH> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """There is at present no scientific consensus or a single agreed-upon theory for how transformative change leading to a sustainable future occurs. Rather, there is a diverse and extensive body of literature that spans many disciplines and areas of study and provides different insights on the process of transformative change and how it is initiated, promoted and scaled (Annex 3.3). Six broad approaches were identified (systems, structural, inner transformations, empowerment, knowledge co-creation and science and technology) and have underlying commonalities in their views of how transformative change occurs and the key actions and actors that can bring about change (Section 3.2). Each of the approaches can be linked to particular focal entry points of views, structures, practices or combinations of these (Figure 3.1). There is currently no “meta-theory” of transformative change that synthesizes across the six approaches (Section 3.7). However, a one-dimensional approach to transformative change is insufficient and there is no single best approach to initiating transformative change. Rather, promoting and accelerating transformative change is more likely when the different approaches are used in combinations that support one another and are suited to a particular context and point in time of a transformative change process.

Historical examples show that transformative change on the scale needed to address biodiversity loss and nature’s decline has occurred multiple times throughout history and is possible. Although most of these historic changes have been detrimental to biodiversity (e.g., Industrial Revolution) (Box 3.3), they provide important insights about the dynamics and processes of the fundamental, system-wide transformative change needed to halt the loss of biodiversity and nature’s decline. At the same time, ongoing initiatives from around the world provide promising examples of transformative change at local to regional scales that have the potential to inform and contribute to system-wide change at the scale needed to halt the loss of biodiversity and nature’s decline (Section 3.4, Box 3.1).

These examples emphasize that transformative change is rarely the outcome of a single event or driver and is better understood as a pathway or process of change involving multiple cascading changes that trigger and reinforce one another, often in unexpected ways (Chapter 1; Sections 3.2 and 3.3; Box 3.1).

This section synthesizes key insights about how transformative change occurs, as evidenced by the six broad approaches to transformative change, historical case studies of transformative change and insights from the database of local and regional examples.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key insights on how transformative change occurs" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/TCA24-KM7> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
