<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/TCA24ch3> a ipbes:Chapter ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:TCA24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Chapter 3 assesses the literature and a set of cases on how transformative change occurs. Based on this assessment, it identifies six broad approaches to transformative change and synthesizes key insights for achieving the 2050 Vision for Biodiversity and global sustainability. In combination, these six approaches can promote, accelerate and scale transformative change for global sustainability in diverse contexts. Chapter 3 recognizes that transformative change takes place in a complex world and that navigating such change calls for adaptability, agility, transparency, continuous monitoring, evaluation and adaptive learning across multiple scales. Other elements for navigating transformative change are delineated by the findings of the assessment below.

1. Theories from diverse disciplinary perspectives, historical examples of broad-scale transformation, as well as a multitude of ongoing initiatives that aim to build a more equitable and sustainable world show that deliberate transformative change is possible (established but incomplete) {3.1, 3.2, 3.4, 3.5}. These theories, examples and initiatives highlight a diversity of approaches and pathways to promote, accelerate and scale transformative change, involving different approaches and combinations of approaches depending on the context and phase of the transformative change process (Box 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, and 3.4) {3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5}.

2. Transformative change is a complex and multi-faceted process, involving interlinked changes across sectors and scales, diverse feedbacks and the potential for unexpected and unintended outcomes (well established) {3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5}. Interventions and changes such as new technologies, policies and practices affect multiple aspects of a system, often in unpredictable ways and with long-term effects (Box 3.1, 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4) {3.2, 3.5}. It is important to pay special attention to feedback mechanisms across different levels and scales, as these are particularly likely to result in unexpected and unintended outcomes {3.2.1, 3.5.7}.

3. Many knowledge systems, including Indigenous and local knowledge and expert knowledge from a wide range of academic disciplines, provide insights on how transformative change occurs and how it can be promoted or how to navigate it (well established) {3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5}. No single theory or approach provides a sufficient or complete understanding of the complexity of transformative change and how to achieve it across different contexts {3.3, 3.5}. Weaving together a variety of knowledge systems leads to a more comprehensive understanding of the potential approaches and combinations of approaches that are useful for initiating and navigating transformative change, as different knowledges provide different perspectives and highlight different approaches to transformation {3.2, 3.3, 3.5, 3.5.1, 3.5.2}. Indigenous and local knowledge systems have particularly rich insights to offer, given their long historical foundations and deep relationships with nature (Box 3.1 and 3.2) {3.2, 3.5.2, 3.5.3}.

4. Drawing on an extensive and robust evaluation of the evidence base, including gaps, this assessment identified six broad approaches to transformative change that highlight different entry points, actors and actions to promote, accelerate and scale transformative change (well established) {3.2, 3.3}. Each approach emphasizes views, structures or practices, or specific combinations of these, as key entry points for initiating and navigating transformative change {3.2, 3.3}. Each approach includes a variety of theories, frameworks, methods and areas of study that have commonalities in their underlying assumptions and understandings of the root causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline, how transformative change occurs and their implications for policy and practice {3.2, 3.3}. These approaches can complement each other and are often most effective when used in combinations {3.2, 3.3, 3.5}. Indigenous and local knowledge features in and makes important contributions to all these approaches (Table 3.1, Figure 3.3) {3.2, 3.5}, which are listed below: 1) Systems approach: According to this approach, transformative change involves systemic shifts that reshape the relationships between system parts and human-environment interactions (Table 3.1) {3.2.1}. The systems approach, with its attention to relationships between people and nature as system components, is aligned with historically-rooted Indigenous and local knowledge which includes the recognition of people-nature interconnectedness {3.2.1}. This approach emphasizes the interplay between structures and practices in bringing about transformative change (Figure 3.1). This approach involves attention to features such as actor groups from different sectors, their strategies, practices and structures that shape a system and its outcomes, especially recognizing the feedbacks that govern the relationships between system components {3.2.1}. Key actions include those that alter the relationships and feedbacks that block transformative change or can help accelerate systemic change, including changes to the structure, rules and networks in a system and the overall goals or underlying intent of the system. Key actors include social entrepreneurs, grassroot movements, Indigenous Peoples and local communities, non-governmental organizations, the private sector and governments and policymakers (Table 3.1) {3.2.1}. 2) Structural approach: According to the structural approach, transformative change occurs when there are shifts in economic, cultural, political or social structures (e.g., institutions, rules and norms) to promote sustainability {3.2.2} (Table 3.1). This approach emphasizes the role of structures as a key entry point for transformative change (Figure 3.1). Key actions are those that change social, economic, legal and political rules and key actors are organizations and actors with the capacity to make these changes (Table 3.1) {3.2.2}. The institutionalization of equitable use and preservation of Indigenous and local knowledge (e.g., the free, prior and informed consent of Indigenous Peoples for activities undertaken on their land, as detailed in the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples) is an important example of a structural approach to change {3.2.2}. 3) Inner transformation approach: According to the inner transformation approach, transformative change occurs through integrative measures that link individual and collective inner worlds (such as mindsets, beliefs, values, worldviews, paradigms, cultures and associated inner cognitive, emotional and relational capacities) with exterior worlds (such as habits, practices, reactions, responses, governance, welfare) (Table 3.1) {3.2.3}. This approach emphasizes the role of views as a key entry point for transformative change (Figure 3.1). The key actors under this approach are any individuals and groups that promote reconnection of people to one another and to nature, often involving historically marginalized groups such as Indigenous Peoples and local communities, women and youth. This approach recognizes that the potential to enable transformative change is latent within everyone. Key actions include those that seek to reestablish more harmonious and symbiotic human-nature relationships, moving away from relationships characterized by extraction and control of nature (Table 3.1) {3.2.3}. 4) Empowerment approach: The empowerment approach emphasizes that transformative change occurs when marginalized actors and groups exercise their agency and power to change power relations for a more just and sustainable world {3.2.4}. This approach emphasizes the interplay between structures and views in bringing about transformative change (Figure 3.1), for instance by advocating against historical legacies of colonization and asserting the rights of Indigenous Peoples and local communities, recognizing their autonomy and self-determination. The key actors under this approach are historically marginalized groups and those who can support them in their struggles for representation, recognition and rights. Examples of key actions include those that address power imbalances and related injustices especially around environmental decision-making where people’s rights are at stake (Table 3.1) {3.2.4}. 5) Knowledge co-creation approach: According to this approach, a variety of actors (such as civil society, States and scientific actors, local communities, holders of Indigenous and local knowledge) work together to create transformative change through collaborative processes of knowledge co-creation and building science-society relationships (Table 3.1) {3.2.5}. This approach emphasizes the interplay between views and practices in bringing about transformative change (Figure 3.1). Key actors include individuals, scientists, Indigenous Peoples and local communities, youth, policymakers, non-governmental organizations and the private sector, that undertake collaborative actions such as collaborative knowledge generation, grassroots visioning and other transformative science-society initiatives that couple knowledge to action (Table 3.1) {3.2.5}. 6) Science and technology approach: According to the science and technology approach, transformative change happens when scientific and technological discoveries deliver new developments, perspectives or solutions that are taken up by society and brought to scale (Table 3.1) {3.2.6}. These approaches emphasize the role of innovation, technology and the practice of science for transformative change. Key actors include scientists, researchers, entrepreneurs and policymakers, in some cases in interaction with citizens, marginalized communities and Indigenous and local knowledge holders. Key actions are the development and use of new scientific and technical knowledge to address underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline (Table 3.1) {3.2.6}.

5. The most effective entry points and combinations of approaches for promoting transformation in any situation depend on the underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline in a given political, cultural, social, economic and environmental context, the particular strengths and capacities of the actors and system, along with the beliefs and values that drive people’s vision of a better future situated in that context (established but incomplete) {3.3, 3.5.2}. How actors make sense of the effectiveness and legitimacy of efforts aimed at transformative change depends on which ideologies, worldviews and interests are present in each situation {3.5}. Ignoring contextual factors introduces higher risks that transformative initiatives fail, diverge significantly from their intended outcomes, or create other harmful consequences {3.5.2, 3.5.4}.

6. Transformative change entails trade-offs and tensions to be navigated among actors because of changes in the distribution of costs and benefits that accompany the process of transformative change (well established) {3.2.4, 3.5.4}. Not everyone benefits, or benefits in the same ways, from transformative change, which can lead to significant tensions between those who gain from change and those who bear its costs. Transformative change may be contested by those afforded power in the status quo, who perceive they will be disempowered by the change or lose valued benefits and ways of life, or by those who lack capacity to adapt {3.2.4}. Thus, the relative position of different worldviews, values and power relations, as embedded in wider contexts and across different socio-political and economic trajectories, will influence the outcomes of efforts to promote and navigate transformative change {3.4, 3.5}.

7. Enabling diverse actors and forms of agency builds momentum and contributes to transformative change (well established) {3.3, 3.5.7}. Transformative change is achieved both by reconfiguring existing dominant structures and processes that maintain current unsustainable and inequitable systems and by co-creating new institutions, structures and processes {3.5, 3.5.3}. Collective agency plays an important role in reconfiguring dominant systems and creating more sustainable systems {3.5.7}. Greater collaboration between actors is associated with enhanced consequences for nature’s contributions to people for a just and sustainable world {3.4}. For example, co-designing research between scientists and relevant communities leads to greater potential to attain sustainability via social learning, capacity-building, strengthened sustainability actors or change agent networks and can also improve acceptance of research findings {3.2.5, 3.4}. In addition, self-determination and movement-building by Indigenous Peoples and other communities are often robust features of transformative change, through strategies such as lobbying, direct action, protest, strategic alliances with multiple actors and capacity-building {3.2.4}.

8. Each of the six approaches to transformative change has a role to play in a global transformation (established but incomplete) {3.3}. Interconnecting different approaches and working across multiple scales helps to address multiple dimensions of change and to leverage the contributions of diverse actors, resulting in more effective promotion, acceleration and scaling of transformative change for sustainability (well established) {3.3; 3.5}. A global transformation to sustainability will be composed of multiple interacting pathways and a mosaic of initiatives from a variety of places and at a variety of scales, leading to both expected and unexpected emergent outcomes {3.5.7}. Spatial, temporal and socio-political scales are all intertwined in promoting transformative change and there is no best level or scale to trigger a process of transformative change {3.5.6}. Rather, efforts at different scales can reinforce and amplify one another if aligned, or can work against one another if not. Leveraging the complementarities and synergies among different approaches to transformative change can enable more effective efforts by reinforcing the shifts and avoiding the potential for backsliding and resistance {3.5}. Monitoring and responding adaptively to emergent outcomes and interactions between different initiatives will be essential to successfully navigating transformative change {3.5.7}.""" ;
